// anydb connection profiles -- TEMPLATE
// =====================================
//
// Copy this file to ~/.anydb/db.json, delete the `//` lines, and fill in your
// own values. Two files that make sense:
//
//     POSIX:   ~/.anydb/db.json
//     Windows: %USERPROFILE%\.anydb\db.json
//     or:     $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/anydb/db.json
//     or:     whatever ANYDB_CONFIG points at
//
// This file is JSON *with comments* (JSONC), because JSON itself has none and
// every field below has a decision attached to it. Remove the `//` lines before
// you save it: the loader is `JSON.parse`, and it will refuse a file with a
// comment in it.
//
// PERMISSIONS
// -----------
// `db.json` is a credential store. It must be 0600, and the directory 0700:
//
//     chmod 700 ~/.anydb
//     chmod 600 ~/.anydb/db.json
//
// `db_list` reports a profile's name, description, driver, whether it is the
// default, and whether it is read-only. It never reports a URI, a host, a
// username, a password or a file path -- not masked, absent -- because that
// text goes into the model's context window.
//
// TOP-LEVEL SHAPE
// ---------------
//   default   optional; the profile name an unqualified `profile` resolves to
//             and the one `db_list` lists first. Must name a profile that exists.
//   profiles  required; an object of named connections.
//   $schema   optional; ignored by this server, used by editors.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


{
  "$schema": "https://anydb.dev/schema/db.json",

  // The profile `db_list` lists first.
  "default": "app-readonly",

  "profiles": {

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1. PostgreSQL, password from the environment
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The `{"env": …}` form. The variable is read here, on this side of the
    // JSON-RPC frame, and the value never becomes part of a tool call. An unset
    // or empty variable is a *named* failure, not a silent empty password.
    //
    // Prefer this over a literal password anywhere. A literal is accepted and
    // warned about once per profile, but it makes the file itself the secret.
    "app-readonly": {
      "description": "Application database, read-only. Row counts and schema questions.",
      "driver": "postgres",
      "uri": "postgres://app_ro@db.example.com:5432/appdb",
      "password": { "env": "APP_DB_PASSWORD" },

      // A profile that does not mention a limit does not get one either; the
      // server default (1000 rows, 256 KiB) applies. Pin them here when this
      // particular database deserves a smaller answer.
      "maxRows": 500,
      "maxBytes": 131072,
      "queryTimeoutMs": 15000,

      // Refuse anything outside this host, whatever a caller sends. The profile
      // list and ANYDB_ALLOWED_HOSTS both apply; the tighter one wins. `*` means
      // every host and `*.example.com` means subdomains but not the bare domain.
      "hosts": ["*.example.com"],

      // Schema and table allowlists, for SQL. A HEURISTIC OVER IDENTIFIERS, not
      // a parser and not a security boundary: it reads table names out of the
      // statement text. A dynamically constructed name defeats it. The database
      // role is the real control. With allowedSchemas set, an unqualified table
      // name is refused rather than resolved against an unreadable search_path.
      "allowedSchemas": ["public"]
    },

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2. MySQL / MariaDB, password from a file
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The `{"file": …}` form. One trailing newline is stripped, because every
    // way of writing a secret file ends one. A file larger than 64 KiB is
    // refused, and a file readable beyond its owner is warned about once.
    //
    // `mariadb://` is MariaDB's own scheme name and routes to the MySQL driver.
    "reporting": {
      "description": "Reporting replica. Wide reads, so a bigger row budget and a longer timeout.",
      "driver": "mariadb",
      "uri": "mariadb://report_ro@mysql-replica.example.com:3306/reporting",
      // TLS goes on the connection string, in the spellings this adapter
      // understands: REQUIRED encrypts, VERIFY_CA and VERIFY_IDENTITY also check
      // the certificate. An unrecognised mode is an error, not a silently
      // plaintext connection. To use it, replace the "uri" above with:
      //
      //   "uri": "mariadb://report_ro@mysql-replica.example.com:3306/reporting?ssl-mode=VERIFY_CA&charset=utf8mb4"
      "password": { "file": "/run/secrets/reporting_db_password" },

      "maxRows": 2000,
      "maxBytes": 524288,
      "queryTimeoutMs": 60000,
      "hosts": ["mysql-replica.example.com"]
    },

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3. MongoDB Atlas, password from a command
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The `{"exec": […]}` form. The argv array is run with `execFile` and no
    // shell, so nothing in this file can be turned into a second command; each
    // element is one argument to the child process. stdout is the secret, and
    // it is never logged: a failure message reports the exit code and the
    // reason, deliberately not the output.
    //
    // `mongodb+srv://` is the form Atlas hands out and it is resolved by the
    // driver. The database name must be in the path -- a URI without one is
    // refused, because otherwise every command would go wherever the server
    // happens to default to while the tool reported no database at all.
    //
    // This is also the only writable profile in this file. `readOnly: false`
    // allows a write to existing data. `allowDestructive` is NOT set, so a
    // statement that changes schema or replaces a collection is still refused
    // -- and the two flags are set by the same caller, which is a weak
    // boundary. Give it a database role that cannot write and neither flag
    // matters.
    "atlas-app": {
      "description": "MongoDB Atlas, application collections. The only writable profile here.",
      "driver": "mongodb",
      "uri": "mongodb+srv://app_rw@cluster0.example.mongodb.net/appdb",
      "password": {
        "exec": ["op", "read", "op://services/anydb/atlas/password"],
        "timeoutMs": 5000
      },
      "readOnly": false,
      "maxRows": 200,
      "queryTimeoutMs": 20000,
      "hosts": ["cluster0.example.mongodb.net"]
    },

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4. Redis, password from a keychain
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The `{"keychain": …}` form. There is no cross-platform keychain reader in
    // Node's standard library, so one is not built in: set ANYDB_KEYCHAIN_CMD to
    // a template, or inject a `keychainProvider` when you embed this package.
    //
    //     ANYDB_KEYCHAIN_CMD=op read op://{service}/{account}
    //     ANYDB_KEYCHAIN_CMD=security find-generic-password -s {service} -a {account} -w
    //
    // {service} and {account} are substituted, the template is split on
    // whitespace, and the result is run with `execFile` -- no shell, so a
    // service or account name cannot carry a command. The command template is
    // deliberately NOT configurable from this file: a writable db.json must
    // not be able to choose what this server executes.
    //
    // `rediss://` is redis over TLS. A URI with no username is the normal form
    // for a Redis ACL `default` user, so the username is left out here.
    //
    // NOTE: a Redis URI is pinned to the database its path names. SELECT,
    // MULTI/EXEC and the other session commands are refused, because a cached
    // connection is shared and `SELECT 1` would silently retarget every later
    // command for this URI.
    "cache": {
      "description": "Redis cache. Fast single-key reads.",
      "driver": "redis",
      "uri": "rediss://default:not-a-real-password@redis.example.com:6379/0",
      "password": { "keychain": "anydb/redis", "account": "default" },
      "queryTimeoutMs": 5000,
      "maxRows": 200
    },

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5. SQLite, and the path allowlist
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // `path` is resolved against the directory holding THIS file, not against
    // the server's working directory -- which is set by the MCP client, not by
    // whoever wrote the config. A committed db.json can therefore carry
    // "./data/app.db" and work on every machine that checks it out.
    //
    // `path` is for SQLite only, and only with `"driver": "sqlite"`; a `path`
    // on any other driver is a load-time error rather than a silently ignored
    // field. A `password` alongside a `path` is refused: a SQLite file is
    // opened with this process's filesystem permissions and has no credentials.
    //
    // `allowedPaths` is a directory allowlist for this profile. A path matches
    // only when it IS the directory or sits inside it on a path-segment
    // boundary, so "C:/data/app2.db" does not match "C:/data/app.db".
    //
    // Read the SQLite section of docs/security.md before you rely on any of
    // this: by default a sqlite:// URI can open any file this process can
    // read, as a database. ANYDB_STRICT_SQLITE_PATHS=1 turns the default
    // around so a path has to be named in advance.
    "dev": {
      "description": "Local development database. Read-only, and only the two directories below.",
      "driver": "sqlite",
      "path": "./data/app.db",
      "readOnly": true,
      "allowedPaths": ["./data"],
      "queryTimeoutMs": 5000
    }
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A LITERAL PASSWORD IS ALSO ACCEPTED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//     "password": "some-plaintext-password"
//
// It is the ordinary case -- a password in a 0600 file is what ~/.pgpass has
// always been -- and the server warns about it once per profile rather than
// refusing it. But it makes this file a credential: a crash report, a backup, a
// `git add -A`, or a client that reads it and shows it to you all become a leak.
// The four reference forms above are the answer to that, and they are the shapes
// 1Password and AWS converged on for the same reason.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A FOURTH THING THIS FILE CANNOT DO
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This file is writable by anyone who can write files, and `description`,
// `allowedSchemas` and the profile names go into the model's context window.
// So a profile description is a prompt-injection surface, which is why its
// length is capped (500 characters) and list sizes are capped. The real answer
// is filesystem permissions: 0600 on the file, 0700 on the directory, and an
// account the agent's user cannot write.
